1
00:00:02,774 --> 00:00:17,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I thought maybe you could say something about the parallels or similarities between this sort of structure of the sattva-kathānas and jhāna.

2
00:00:17,959 --> 00:00:21,600
[SPEAKER_02]: Just something you've been searching for.

3
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:31,783
[SPEAKER_01]: I think the most obvious thing is to, as we've repeated a countless amount of times,

4
00:00:32,217 --> 00:00:37,038
[SPEAKER_01]: In, eh... in our talks is the, eh... mindfulness.

5
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:39,439
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's what the common thread is.

6
00:00:39,479 --> 00:00:41,019
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically what samādhi is.

7
00:00:41,859 --> 00:00:43,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness that is properly established.

8
00:00:46,701 --> 00:01:00,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you're mindful through, eh... through satipaṭṭhānas, mindful of your body, you can't turn that mindfulness, then, on into practice of the jhāna if you start, as Buddha said, if you start discerning the factors of the jhāna, what are the things necessary for the jhāna.

9
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:04,122
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh...

10
00:01:05,643 --> 00:01:06,483
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same principle.

11
00:01:06,684 --> 00:01:07,544
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle.

12
00:01:07,564 --> 00:01:10,606
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I wouldn't necessarily go, oh, this is what's similar, this is what's parallel.

13
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:25,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, if you have mindfulness established to the certain necessary extent, then if you direct it in the way that the Buddha described what the first jhāna is, that's how you enter the first jhāna.

14
00:01:25,954 --> 00:01:28,836
[SPEAKER_01]: And what we spoke before, again, it's a figure of speech.

15
00:01:28,876 --> 00:01:31,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't really enter the first jhāna as such.

16
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:34,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like waiting for you and then you do something and then you enter it.

17
00:01:35,831 --> 00:01:40,112
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like... you dissolve yourself into it.

18
00:01:41,813 --> 00:01:51,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, jhāna is a state of... jhāna is a state of being.

19
00:01:53,036 --> 00:01:53,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

20
00:01:53,456 --> 00:01:57,057
[SPEAKER_01]: State of being that's above the state of central being.

21
00:01:58,883 --> 00:02:06,088
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sort of... That's why he said he dwells, he lives in jhāna, having developed it beforehand.

22
00:02:06,148 --> 00:02:06,929
[SPEAKER_01]: A mode of existence.

23
00:02:07,149 --> 00:02:08,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mode of existence.

24
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:09,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, imagine your day-to-day life.

25
00:02:09,651 --> 00:02:15,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you are... and there is a certain level of is-ness, so to speak, to the things.

26
00:02:16,036 --> 00:02:21,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the is-ness of a mind who is not in jhāna, a mind not developed through wisdom, is is-ness of central domain.

27
00:02:23,222 --> 00:02:32,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although you might be practicing restraint in regard to sensuality, you are still within sensual domain, which means you're still within the domain of your senses and sense bases.

28
00:02:32,651 --> 00:02:33,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything that is...

29
00:02:34,732 --> 00:02:36,154
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within that.

30
00:02:36,174 --> 00:02:42,359
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have a choice to pursue sensuality or restrain, but it's still within the sensual being.

31
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:44,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the karmabhāva.

32
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:46,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the existence of humans.

33
00:02:46,703 --> 00:02:48,004
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the human existence, exactly.

34
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:49,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to make the effort.

35
00:02:50,088 --> 00:02:58,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I'll replace this existence with something else, because that will kind of require you... imply that your point of view remains unchanged.

36
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:06,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to dissolve the existence of kāma-bhāva, and then what you have in return is existence that's above sensuality, which is what the first jhāna is.

37
00:03:07,132 --> 00:03:08,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Dissolve it by understanding it.

38
00:03:08,212 --> 00:03:08,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

39
00:03:08,552 --> 00:03:09,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what the Buddha described.

40
00:03:09,572 --> 00:03:15,954
[SPEAKER_01]: He comes in, sets up his mindfulness, enters the jhāna, and then whether he sits, extends his arm, goes to the toilet, eats,

41
00:03:17,029 --> 00:03:19,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Lays down, he's in that divine state of that jhāna.

42
00:03:20,991 --> 00:03:25,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not something you enter that absorbs you and then you kind of come out of it.

43
00:03:25,573 --> 00:03:27,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It really becomes your mode of being.

44
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:28,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it says.

45
00:03:28,734 --> 00:03:39,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Having abandoned sensuality beforehand, having abandoned concern, doubt, restlessness, grief in regard to the world beforehand, you start experiencing pleasure on account of that abandonment.

46
00:03:40,559 --> 00:03:42,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you start living in the jhāna.

47
00:03:44,762 --> 00:03:47,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it is a pleasant abiding here and now.

48
00:03:47,404 --> 00:03:49,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, pleasant living here and now.

49
00:03:49,105 --> 00:03:49,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is pleasant.

50
00:03:50,466 --> 00:04:03,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And in another sutta, incidentally, Root of the Tree, it was when Anuradha, the Buddha, was encouraging him to practice jhāna, and he said, living under a root of a tree will feel like king's palace if you develop your jhāna.

51
00:04:04,357 --> 00:04:05,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like that.

52
00:04:05,518 --> 00:04:06,759
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know where he is.

53
00:04:06,819 --> 00:04:09,461
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows exactly, but everything starts feeling

54
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:11,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Delightful, so to speak.

55
00:04:11,991 --> 00:04:16,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is completely outside of the central domain and that's where the pleasure originates from.

56
00:04:16,913 --> 00:04:17,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom.

57
00:04:17,553 --> 00:04:18,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from burden.

58
00:04:18,854 --> 00:04:20,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he goes on and saying...

59
00:04:20,814 --> 00:04:23,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, living under the root of a tree will feel like a king's palace.

60
00:04:23,356 --> 00:04:29,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Wearing rough rag, hemp type of robes will feel like Kashmir silt.

61
00:04:29,918 --> 00:04:35,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Fermented cow urine and feces will feel like honey and ghee and butter.

62
00:04:37,281 --> 00:04:38,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the jhāna.

63
00:04:38,989 --> 00:04:40,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... what was the other one?

64
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:41,611
[SPEAKER_01]: The rice.

65
00:04:42,211 --> 00:04:42,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

66
00:04:42,591 --> 00:04:46,754
[SPEAKER_01]: The rough food he gets in Pittaburgh, we feel like the rice served by the kings and curries.

67
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:48,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you... like...

68
00:04:50,016 --> 00:04:52,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the same horrible rice that he eats.

69
00:04:52,638 --> 00:04:54,519
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the same rough robes that he wears.

70
00:04:55,519 --> 00:04:57,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's felt divine.

71
00:04:57,781 --> 00:05:04,705
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like pure source of joy that you cannot even fathom, because you're still within the central being.

72
00:05:05,445 --> 00:05:07,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you withdraw yourself from the central being,

73
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:12,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever, wherever you are, you're unburdened, you're free.

74
00:05:12,572 --> 00:05:25,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's again many stories in the suttas where the Buddha would be sleeping by the road on that pile of leaves, and a man walks past and says, well, whoever slept here last night must have had a rough night.

75
00:05:26,298 --> 00:05:30,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And Buddha said, no, among those who sleep peacefully, I'm the foremost in sleeping peacefully.

76
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:32,941
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he gives him the teaching on account of it.

77
00:05:32,961 --> 00:05:36,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, the roughness, the discomfort just

78
00:05:36,781 --> 00:05:39,143
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you're unaware of it, what's happening.

79
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:40,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You're aware of it, and it's pleasant.

80
00:05:40,744 --> 00:05:41,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, right.

81
00:05:42,005 --> 00:05:42,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pleasant.

82
00:05:42,625 --> 00:05:47,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, what is truly pleasant is determined whether your mind is gripped by the central being or not.

83
00:05:48,990 --> 00:05:50,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Things just are in a different way.

84
00:05:51,111 --> 00:05:51,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

85
00:05:51,832 --> 00:05:55,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Same things, externally, but they're lived differently now.

86
00:05:56,936 --> 00:05:57,776
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what jhāna is.

87
00:05:57,997 --> 00:05:58,937
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what first jhāna is.

88
00:05:58,997 --> 00:06:00,258
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that's enough for arahantship.

89
00:06:00,478 --> 00:06:02,158
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, you can take that further as well.

90
00:06:02,859 --> 00:06:04,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Second jhāna, third jhāna and so on.

91
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:08,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same principle of same undoing, of same withdrawal of being into a greater being.

92
00:06:09,602 --> 00:06:11,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Into a more purified form of being.

93
00:06:13,023 --> 00:06:16,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you really want to enter jhāna, you need to first understand what your current being is.

94
00:06:16,505 --> 00:06:20,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like try to escape from the current central being through attaining the jhāna.

95
00:06:22,189 --> 00:06:26,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Through understanding the extent of central domain, you free yourself from central domain.

96
00:06:26,851 --> 00:06:29,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You experience, then, joy on account of that freedom.

97
00:06:40,456 --> 00:06:48,379
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking in terms of... so, first jhāna, or like... You might want to speak up a bit.

98
00:06:48,399 --> 00:06:48,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the...

99
00:06:52,325 --> 00:07:02,193
[SPEAKER_02]: The satipaṭṭhāna is sort of, you know, described as one sees the body within the body.

100
00:07:04,355 --> 00:07:13,662
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a kind of seeing something within a context, an outline, like a limit.

101
00:07:15,294 --> 00:07:16,134
[SPEAKER_02]: Something that limits.

102
00:07:16,235 --> 00:07:16,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

103
00:07:17,215 --> 00:07:17,695
[SPEAKER_02]: A container.

104
00:07:17,835 --> 00:07:18,336
[SPEAKER_02]: A container.

105
00:07:19,997 --> 00:07:22,738
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I was thinking of.

106
00:07:22,778 --> 00:07:27,561
[SPEAKER_02]: That same structure applied to jhāna.

107
00:07:27,761 --> 00:07:28,541
[SPEAKER_02]: That's also there.

108
00:07:29,462 --> 00:07:29,722
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

109
00:07:30,002 --> 00:07:31,503
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

110
00:07:31,523 --> 00:07:44,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding the thinking and pondering within the... Understanding the thinking and pondering, for example, within the central domain.

111
00:07:45,872 --> 00:07:47,712
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to surmount the sensual domain.

112
00:07:47,952 --> 00:07:50,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no sensuality without thinking and pondering.

113
00:07:50,433 --> 00:07:52,273
[SPEAKER_02]: But there can be thinking and pondering without sensuality.

114
00:07:54,594 --> 00:07:59,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, even your sensuality is your thinking and pondering.

115
00:07:59,094 --> 00:08:03,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding thinking and pondering within thinking and pondering.

116
00:08:03,475 --> 00:08:10,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Realize that it doesn't matter even when you engage with sensuality, you're still within the container of your thoughts of sensuality.

117
00:08:10,577 --> 00:08:12,197
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality never leaves your mind.

118
00:08:13,088 --> 00:08:13,788
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

119
00:08:13,808 --> 00:08:16,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is not in beautiful things.

120
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:19,352
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within you and always stays within you.

121
00:08:19,932 --> 00:08:27,857
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's you start experiencing the beautiful things or ugly things within the framework of your central mind, which is nothing other than your central thoughts.

122
00:08:28,477 --> 00:08:33,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you learn to discern that thoughts need to be there in order for them to be central or otherwise.

123
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:36,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get to understand on account of central thoughts the nature of thoughts.

124
00:08:36,682 --> 00:08:38,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you overcome the central thoughts.

125
00:08:40,829 --> 00:08:50,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, in the same sense, you get to understand the nature of the body as a necessary container for any sense to engage with, either for pleasure or for pain.

126
00:08:50,751 --> 00:08:52,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what you're exposed to.

127
00:08:52,572 --> 00:08:56,752
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to always be within the container of the body.

128
00:08:56,772 --> 00:08:58,353
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be outside of it.

129
00:08:58,393 --> 00:08:59,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the same...

130
00:09:00,033 --> 00:09:01,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's discerning the body within the body...

131
00:09:01,954 --> 00:09:04,374
[SPEAKER_01]: But that, otherwise, just means discerning the body in the right order.

132
00:09:04,594 --> 00:09:04,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

133
00:09:05,194 --> 00:09:07,935
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, so, kāyāna-pāsanādhi,

134
00:09:11,195 --> 00:09:28,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the body, within the body, is basically recognizing, in whatever way the body is manifested, that's within the body, it's already there.

135
00:09:28,261 --> 00:09:28,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

136
00:09:29,141 --> 00:09:30,902
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowing that...

137
00:09:30,922 --> 00:09:34,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing the nature of the body, knowing the necessary framework, having that peripheral

138
00:09:35,875 --> 00:09:41,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognition of what the body is, regardless of what... of how, currently, that body is manifesting.

139
00:09:41,259 --> 00:09:50,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get to see this body within the body, within the nature of the body, that kind of remains always the same, unchanging, because it's always seen on that peripheral level.

140
00:09:53,628 --> 00:09:57,611
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, like, even... even when the Buddha encourages contemplating the aggregates,

141
00:09:58,968 --> 00:10:01,089
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be again the same principle, the same answer.

142
00:10:01,169 --> 00:10:06,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if far or near, if there is a feeling or matter that you're experiencing, whatever.

143
00:10:06,913 --> 00:10:12,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Far or near, big or small, personal, impersonal, inside out, feeling is there.

144
00:10:13,217 --> 00:10:14,538
[SPEAKER_01]: As in the feeling...

145
00:10:14,638 --> 00:10:20,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever particular feeling there is, mine, others, this one, that one, that one, that one, that one, that one, feeling is there.

146
00:10:20,082 --> 00:10:23,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And this particular feeling is seen within the feeling being there.

147
00:10:23,144 --> 00:10:24,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling within the feeling.

148
00:10:25,954 --> 00:10:29,855
[SPEAKER_01]: And by seeing it that way, you realize, oh, it's just two points of view on the same feeling.

149
00:10:30,836 --> 00:10:37,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And you kept gratuitously assuming yourself to be somehow in between these two, like a non-defined, ambiguous center.

150
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:44,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you were automatically regarded yourself as the owner of your feelings.

151
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you look at the feeling, you only get to see the feeling.

152
00:10:49,101 --> 00:10:51,062
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at the perception, you only get to see perception.

153
00:10:51,612 --> 00:10:53,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the bhāgī is sutta.

154
00:10:53,413 --> 00:10:54,913
[SPEAKER_01]: In the scene, let there just be the scene.

155
00:10:55,473 --> 00:10:57,554
[SPEAKER_01]: He could have said, in the felt, let there just be the felt.

156
00:10:58,434 --> 00:11:01,335
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, not just, oh, just pretend there's no problem with what's felt.

157
00:11:01,375 --> 00:11:01,556
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

158
00:11:01,796 --> 00:11:02,896
[SPEAKER_01]: See felt within the felt.

159
00:11:03,736 --> 00:11:06,617
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you realize that picture does not require you or your own opinion.

160
00:11:07,518 --> 00:11:08,418
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no room for it.

161
00:11:19,389 --> 00:11:26,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the body is the necessary basis for everything.

162
00:11:26,656 --> 00:11:30,879
[SPEAKER_02]: This particular way in which the body is manifested, or the feelings it's enduring.

163
00:11:33,922 --> 00:11:45,972
[SPEAKER_02]: In jhāna, first jhāna, it's still the case that everything is contained within

164
00:11:48,852 --> 00:11:53,814
[SPEAKER_02]: The body, you're still using the body as the... You can, yeah.

165
00:11:53,834 --> 00:12:00,596
[SPEAKER_02]: But that body is now specifically, more specifically, manifested in the mouth.

166
00:12:03,337 --> 00:12:06,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, more like your relationship with that body.

167
00:12:06,658 --> 00:12:06,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

168
00:12:07,459 --> 00:12:16,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, between your point of view and the body as the means of engagement, gets more specifically around the mouth.

169
00:12:18,507 --> 00:12:18,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

170
00:12:18,767 --> 00:12:22,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Because once you pass the mouth, you're back onto the senses.

171
00:12:23,452 --> 00:12:27,876
[SPEAKER_01]: You're back onto the hands, groin, eyes, still mouth, nose, ears.

172
00:12:28,497 --> 00:12:40,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you start seeing the body as a whole, as a necessary container, you realize that, okay, so you are kind of... your first point of taking up your body is your mouth, since the day you came out of the womb, pretty much.

173
00:12:40,917 --> 00:12:44,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not incidental that all that kind of revolves for baby, you know, putting things in its mouth.

174
00:12:44,759 --> 00:12:46,460
[SPEAKER_01]: That's its way of relating with the world.

175
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:47,741
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how it works.

176
00:12:48,141 --> 00:12:52,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And that always stays, that framework stays, even when you become an adult.

177
00:12:52,484 --> 00:13:07,452
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you proliferate in different ways, in different ways of consuming things, so to speak, with your eyes, with your hands, but still, it's all like a representation of your relationship with your mouth, as in your relationship with your body on a more general level than the level of the specific senses you got used to.

178
00:13:08,299 --> 00:13:10,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sense-restraint is absolutely not optional.

179
00:13:11,020 --> 00:13:14,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I keep saying that, because people say, oh, well, lay people can have jhāna.

180
00:13:14,062 --> 00:13:14,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, of course.

181
00:13:15,442 --> 00:13:19,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if you wear robes or if you wear jeans, that doesn't determine jhāna.

182
00:13:19,644 --> 00:13:21,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you cannot have is sensuality.

183
00:13:22,785 --> 00:13:28,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, lay people usually are the ones who don't restrain themselves from sensuality, because the lay life is about sensuality.

184
00:13:28,568 --> 00:13:29,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can't have the jhāna.

185
00:13:29,968 --> 00:13:33,290
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not because you're a layman, but because you're still not withdrawn from your senses.

186
00:13:35,302 --> 00:13:39,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be withdrawn from the senses, and I don't mean for a week.

187
00:13:39,664 --> 00:13:44,987
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be withdrawn sufficiently enough that, basically, your being gets remolded.

188
00:13:46,728 --> 00:13:48,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Your state of being is now different.

189
00:13:49,209 --> 00:13:53,411
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a state of, oh, I'm just temporarily saying no to this in order to get jhānas.

190
00:13:53,911 --> 00:13:54,372
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

191
00:13:54,952 --> 00:14:00,835
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be so established that, like, your saying no to senses is your normal state of being now.

192
00:14:01,462 --> 00:14:04,563
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires you to make effort to go back to senses.

193
00:14:04,803 --> 00:14:07,123
[SPEAKER_01]: This doesn't require any more effort, because it became your norm.

194
00:14:07,623 --> 00:14:09,344
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you're ready to practice the jhāna.

195
00:14:10,284 --> 00:14:14,585
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it has been... His behavior has been thoroughly developed beforehand.

196
00:14:14,625 --> 00:14:19,746
[SPEAKER_01]: He has abandoned the sensuality and the grief in regard to the world way beforehand, and experienced pleasure on account of it.

197
00:14:20,126 --> 00:14:22,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, then his mind gets composed in jhāna.

198
00:14:28,027 --> 00:14:30,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's said in the first jhāna, back to what you said,

199
00:14:30,841 --> 00:14:31,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, the body is still there.

200
00:14:31,702 --> 00:14:32,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

201
00:14:32,663 --> 00:14:36,547
[SPEAKER_01]: It's said in that sutra, one by one, to Sariputti, Manjulnikaya.

202
00:14:37,007 --> 00:14:43,154
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, he describes the usual factors of the first jhāna, thinking, pondering, so on, and then says, contact was there.

203
00:14:43,334 --> 00:14:43,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

204
00:14:43,915 --> 00:14:44,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception was there.

205
00:14:45,016 --> 00:14:47,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, intention, attention, that was all there.

206
00:14:47,198 --> 00:14:47,338
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

207
00:14:47,598 --> 00:14:50,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's what Putujan's experience has as well.

208
00:14:50,782 --> 00:14:52,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing that wasn't there was sensual being.

209
00:14:53,582 --> 00:14:56,303
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, my experience gets different.

210
00:14:56,343 --> 00:14:58,144
[SPEAKER_01]: My experience gets purified.

211
00:14:58,184 --> 00:15:01,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same framework of the same experience.

212
00:15:01,045 --> 00:15:07,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how Sariputta would know that there is more of this development before he gets the development.

213
00:15:07,948 --> 00:15:10,409
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't get the second jhāna and then say, oh, yes, there is more.

214
00:15:10,449 --> 00:15:11,389
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say, oh, is there more?

215
00:15:11,709 --> 00:15:12,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, there is more.

216
00:15:12,730 --> 00:15:14,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he would develop more and confirm that there is more.

217
00:15:15,231 --> 00:15:17,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize the same framework still carries on.

218
00:15:18,292 --> 00:15:21,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, he basically was asking himself how far I can take this.

219
00:15:21,570 --> 00:15:24,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And you took it all the way up to the sensation of perception and feeling.

220
00:15:28,214 --> 00:15:29,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what I mean.

221
00:15:29,055 --> 00:15:34,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your state of being now, the framework of it stays the same in the jhāna.

222
00:15:35,739 --> 00:15:38,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Minus the sensuality and pressure of the senses, organs.

223
00:15:39,821 --> 00:15:41,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why everything becomes delightful.

224
00:15:41,382 --> 00:15:45,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything is light, free, not pressuring completely.

225
00:15:47,004 --> 00:15:48,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Literal, like, perfect equanimity.

226
00:15:49,426 --> 00:15:51,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, whichever way this goes, it's going to be pleasurable.

227
00:15:53,149 --> 00:15:56,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Not if I get it, but then if I don't, so there's an implicit risk.

228
00:15:56,111 --> 00:15:56,552
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just gone.

229
00:15:56,692 --> 00:16:00,715
[SPEAKER_01]: The entire domain of that kind of dependence and exposure, gone.

230
00:16:00,955 --> 00:16:02,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Entirely gone.

231
00:16:04,238 --> 00:16:05,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha described it.

232
00:16:05,398 --> 00:16:05,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

233
00:16:05,779 --> 00:16:06,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Attention is still there.

234
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:07,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Contact is still there.

235
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:08,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is still there.

236
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:09,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is still there.

237
00:16:09,902 --> 00:16:11,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And perfect mindfulness is still there.

238
00:16:15,146 --> 00:16:16,207
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that he went into, like, a

239
00:16:16,624 --> 00:16:21,126
[SPEAKER_01]: You enter first jhāna, you enter like a transcendental state, outside of your aggregates or something.

240
00:16:22,926 --> 00:16:24,007
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's more like you fell asleep.

241
00:16:24,447 --> 00:16:24,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

242
00:16:25,087 --> 00:16:25,807
[SPEAKER_01]: And drank your bowel.

243
00:16:32,310 --> 00:16:33,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So... How about jhāna?

244
00:16:34,568 --> 00:16:46,540
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to satipaṭṭhāna and jhāna, I always had taken it that, like, satipaṭṭhāna was the grounds for recollection, recollecting oneself.

245
00:16:47,721 --> 00:16:50,324
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, and in regard to jhāna,

246
00:16:51,204 --> 00:17:02,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's that recollectedness of the body and the mind, there's not that... there can be no being scattered amongst views in regard to the experience of jhāna, for the feelings.

247
00:17:02,473 --> 00:17:04,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, I mean, jhāna is connected to feeling.

248
00:17:06,313 --> 00:17:09,015
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sense that it's supposed to be...

249
00:17:09,256 --> 00:17:19,524
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember in the Mahābhūgakaṇḍa Sūtra, the foremost feeling, the gratification of feelings, it said, is jhāna.

250
00:17:19,544 --> 00:17:21,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there are things that are felt in jhāna.

251
00:17:22,526 --> 00:17:24,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:17:24,288 --> 00:17:26,710
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, you will feel delightful on account of any feeling.

253
00:17:27,951 --> 00:17:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like, oh, feeling, normal feeling is here, and sometimes I connect my normal feeling to the jhāna.

254
00:17:34,205 --> 00:17:36,406
[SPEAKER_01]: It's undoing what normal feeling means for you.

255
00:17:36,726 --> 00:17:41,769
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get closer to the feeling of jhāna, to the feeling of renunciation, to the pleasure of renunciation.

256
00:17:43,950 --> 00:17:46,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, see, they said, oh, satipaṭṭhāna is the basis.

257
00:17:46,511 --> 00:17:48,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, satipaṭṭhāna is the basis and stays the basis.

258
00:17:49,352 --> 00:17:51,813
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't reintroduce something new then within satipaṭṭhāna.

259
00:17:52,434 --> 00:17:53,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the same satipaṭṭhāna.

260
00:17:54,515 --> 00:17:56,996
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the same principle of the same mindfulness.

261
00:17:58,333 --> 00:18:07,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Just instead of being anchored with seeing body within the body, you see thought within a thought, or you see mouth within the mouth, as the point of engagement with the body.

262
00:18:08,777 --> 00:18:09,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the jhāna.

263
00:18:10,998 --> 00:18:12,038
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same mindfulness.

264
00:18:18,541 --> 00:18:20,802
[SPEAKER_02]: It's still a kind of peripheral awareness.

265
00:18:21,922 --> 00:18:23,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, same principle, yeah.

266
00:18:23,823 --> 00:18:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as you said before, it's not like, you know, people would think,

267
00:18:27,292 --> 00:18:37,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Jhāna is the meditative experience, and then, oh, I have to be careful, because, you know, I might not be able to handle the meditative experience, or I can get it wrongly, or something.

268
00:18:37,796 --> 00:18:38,716
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

269
00:18:38,736 --> 00:18:42,557
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're getting it wrongly, that's not the jhāna, because it's not... you can't get the jhāna.

270
00:18:43,978 --> 00:18:50,700
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you can know it, and then, knowingly, you can obtain it, or rather, establish it at will, as the Buddha would say.

271
00:18:51,521 --> 00:18:55,642
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, at will, you can withdraw your mind, or keep it withdrawn from the entire central being.

272
00:18:57,458 --> 00:18:58,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... and that's where jhāna is.

273
00:18:59,559 --> 00:19:05,803
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can keep your mind withdrawn from the central being, but then end up in company, having to talk, having to engage with people.

274
00:19:05,823 --> 00:19:16,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might not have the factors of the jhāna enduring at the time, but the necessary basis, the container of withdrawal from sensuality developed beforehand, from... on account of which you experience pleasure, that's still there.

275
00:19:17,670 --> 00:19:25,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why once your engagement stops, the seclusion is restored, the mind just goes back to jhāna, to the being in jhāna.

276
00:19:26,052 --> 00:19:27,253
[SPEAKER_01]: vihārati is the Pali word.

277
00:19:27,273 --> 00:19:29,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwelling in jhāna literally means living in.

278
00:19:29,774 --> 00:19:31,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these days, he lives in jhāna.

279
00:19:31,915 --> 00:19:33,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, these days, he lives in such and such house.

280
00:19:34,836 --> 00:19:36,197
[SPEAKER_01]: These days, he lives in jhāna.

281
00:19:36,337 --> 00:19:38,718
[SPEAKER_01]: He eats, goes to toilet, sleeps in jhāna.

282
00:19:39,639 --> 00:19:41,200
[SPEAKER_01]: That is his mode of being.

283
00:19:42,860 --> 00:19:44,561
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't get my mode of being wrongly.

284
00:19:45,542 --> 00:19:49,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, not getting the jhāna rightly, you're already wrong, because you're already within the central domain.

285
00:19:51,193 --> 00:20:02,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And if any meditation you pursue on account of central domain, as a replacement of what you want, of your desires, or whatever else, it's just going to be another extension of your centrality.

286
00:20:02,175 --> 00:20:04,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Extension of... of your craving.

287
00:20:05,435 --> 00:20:12,337
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there is no sense, as Buddha described as well, like, oh, I am entering jhāna, I am in the jhāna, I am leaving jhāna.

288
00:20:13,397 --> 00:20:18,678
[SPEAKER_01]: It's through overcoming that dependence of I on the senses, you establish yourself in the being of jhāna.

289
00:20:18,758 --> 00:20:20,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Good news, but I think there is...

290
00:20:21,554 --> 00:20:24,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you can enter jhāna, but he has to bend on his eye.

291
00:20:32,995 --> 00:20:39,256
[SPEAKER_01]: He might not develop the right view in regard to his I, in regard to his I am, but he can't.

292
00:20:39,276 --> 00:20:40,377
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have that in the jhāna.

293
00:20:40,397 --> 00:20:41,617
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually cessation of that.

294
00:20:41,797 --> 00:20:46,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, I am is defined by the counterpart of your senses, sense bases.

295
00:20:46,658 --> 00:20:49,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am is the necessary by-product of sensual being.

296
00:20:50,459 --> 00:20:55,100
[SPEAKER_01]: It's generated by it, as the one who is opposite of all these varieties.

297
00:20:55,780 --> 00:21:00,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that whole sensory domain... Once you surmount the entire domain, there is no... there is no...

298
00:21:00,724 --> 00:21:01,144
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no space.

299
00:21:01,264 --> 00:21:01,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

300
00:21:01,625 --> 00:21:03,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Symptom of I am will have to cease.

301
00:21:04,167 --> 00:21:07,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, you can go back to sense domain unknowingly.

302
00:21:07,209 --> 00:21:14,875
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how, like, yeah, Tuttujana can get the jhānas if he lived withdrawn from centrality for a sufficient period of time and then understood that withdrawal.

303
00:21:15,775 --> 00:21:18,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But he hasn't necessarily understood the nature of I am.

304
00:21:19,142 --> 00:21:22,185
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of non-ownership, for which you do need some more instruction.

305
00:21:22,626 --> 00:21:32,375
[SPEAKER_01]: But puthujjana, who can enter jhāna, literally, practically, virtually, could probably need maybe one sentence from the Buddha, just to point to the right thing.

306
00:21:32,535 --> 00:21:36,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would... because he's already done all the work of undoing everything unwholesome from his mind.

