339
00:23:32,492 --> 00:23:32,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yep.

340
00:23:34,459 --> 00:23:35,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Did they all have different...

341
00:23:36,299 --> 00:23:44,504
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, because there was different definitions of Brahman for an arahant, and the Buddha had to clarify, this is what an arahant really is.

342
00:23:44,744 --> 00:23:46,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Were there different definitions of what jhāna meant?

343
00:23:46,825 --> 00:23:59,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there are different values, different set of goals, but if they were doing things rightly, they would have been doing it along the framework of jhānas, or those immaterial establishments of the mind, and so on.

344
00:24:00,352 --> 00:24:02,514
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha's previous teachings.

345
00:24:06,639 --> 00:24:13,852
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's structurally, if you were to develop the same principle of mindfulness, that is higher than the jhānas.

346
00:24:15,742 --> 00:24:21,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you can only imagine, then, how much the mind of those wrong-view teachers were developed.

347
00:24:22,607 --> 00:24:26,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, most people today, who are familiar with suttic or not, you wouldn't be able to fade them.

348
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:31,854
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, they're more developed than even monks who've been practicing the Buddha's teaching.

349
00:24:32,394 --> 00:24:35,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Because neither perception nor perception, it's quite a development of the mind.

350
00:24:35,677 --> 00:24:39,139
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a miraculous, spontaneous, mystical attainment.

351
00:24:39,179 --> 00:24:44,704
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the Buddha referred to it, neither perception nor perception, the same one that then he was teaching, just with the right view.

352
00:24:45,384 --> 00:24:47,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not something different they were doing.

