359
00:25:13,340 --> 00:25:17,963
[SPEAKER_01]: He found the enlightenment himself, and again, he cannot not think from that point of view.

360
00:25:18,023 --> 00:25:19,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, I have to teach.

361
00:25:19,904 --> 00:25:20,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Teaching is a hassle.

362
00:25:21,085 --> 00:25:21,965
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he didn't want to do it.

363
00:25:21,985 --> 00:25:25,567
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Supreme Brahma had to ask him three times.

364
00:25:25,607 --> 00:25:29,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he did decide to do it, he wasn't, oh, who do I feel grateful to?

365
00:25:29,349 --> 00:25:31,851
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it was, who'd be the quickest one to learn?

366
00:25:32,979 --> 00:25:38,401
[SPEAKER_01]: And he remembered his previous teacher, because he was teaching, basically, up to the base of neither perception or perception.

367
00:25:38,761 --> 00:25:39,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine that.

368
00:25:39,842 --> 00:25:47,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody can get you to the point of establishing that as your mode of being, when you are in seclusion.

369
00:25:47,084 --> 00:25:50,125
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's virtually just a step shorter than the other.

370
00:25:51,126 --> 00:26:01,390
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha could have probably... same like with Bhakti, he probably would have just told him one sentence and he would have been... That's why he said, it's such a loss for him, that he died, because if he heard me speak,

371
00:26:02,546 --> 00:26:05,868
[SPEAKER_01]: His mind would have been completely liberated for good.

372
00:26:06,449 --> 00:26:07,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Like parinibbāna, right away.

373
00:26:09,250 --> 00:26:13,693
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he was so close to the goal, but then died away without hearing the goal.

374
00:26:13,953 --> 00:26:21,298
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, you know, he's probably reborn in heavens and stuff, but the wrong view will eventually set in through the round of reappearances and so on.

375
00:26:22,439 --> 00:26:24,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, okay, so who's then the next one?

376
00:26:24,180 --> 00:26:26,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, the next one was the one that was teaching slightly lower.

377
00:26:27,102 --> 00:26:29,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in the end, had to settle for the five disciples.

378
00:26:30,621 --> 00:26:36,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, jhānas or any of these meditative attainments, are not attainments in the sense of

379
00:26:56,736 --> 00:26:58,838
[SPEAKER_01]: I got them, now I'm in them, now I'm out them.

380
00:26:58,858 --> 00:27:00,059
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no idea how I came to be.

381
00:27:00,079 --> 00:27:03,621
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically surmounting what you are, that's how you enter it.

382
00:27:03,861 --> 00:27:08,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you enter second jhāna by surmounting the first jhāna, which is what you are at the time.

383
00:27:09,225 --> 00:27:16,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You enter third jhāna by surmounting the second jhāna, which is why you... That's why you won't enter the second jhāna until you settle in the first jhāna, as the Buddha said.

384
00:27:17,171 --> 00:27:21,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're trying to surmount something you're not even familiar with.

385
00:27:21,334 --> 00:27:21,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the

386
00:27:27,425 --> 00:27:54,278
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can we say... so, can you say... what is it that you would bring your attention to, if you are established in seeing the body, then the body, and now you want to enter jhāna,

387
00:27:57,192 --> 00:27:58,132
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same.

388
00:27:58,293 --> 00:28:11,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been living, withdrawn from centrality, withdrawn from any grief and concern regarding the world, as in, you've been investing effort to develop that, because it's not like you just decide, I will not be concerned about the world anymore.

389
00:28:11,418 --> 00:28:18,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been investing effort to surmount that concern, to surmount that central dependence, and to stay in solitude.

390
00:28:19,102 --> 00:28:20,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the necessary conditions.

391
00:28:20,743 --> 00:28:22,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start finding pleasure in that.

392
00:28:22,864 --> 00:28:23,864
[SPEAKER_01]: You start preferring it.

393
00:28:25,073 --> 00:28:35,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you want to develop jhāna, well, you can either attempt to, for example, converging point of your thoughts towards your body, which is where the mouth is.

394
00:28:38,697 --> 00:28:39,398
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's another thing.

395
00:28:39,418 --> 00:28:45,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you associate, like, your point of view, you know, when you're thinking of something, with your head, behind your eyes.

396
00:28:45,960 --> 00:28:46,940
[SPEAKER_01]: That's completely gratuitous.

397
00:28:48,223 --> 00:28:49,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind is not behind your eyes.

398
00:28:50,325 --> 00:28:53,187
[SPEAKER_01]: It's optic nerves and brain matter is behind your eyes.

399
00:28:53,367 --> 00:28:53,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Flesh.

400
00:28:54,508 --> 00:28:56,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you kind of assume you're behind the eyes.

401
00:28:57,030 --> 00:29:00,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you took the body pretty much on the level of the mouth first.

402
00:29:00,713 --> 00:29:03,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Unrestrained people go much lower in terms of how far they take their body.

403
00:29:04,617 --> 00:29:06,698
[SPEAKER_01]: And their being is on that level of their body.

404
00:29:09,484 --> 00:29:09,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice people.

405
00:29:10,264 --> 00:29:14,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people, it's the head, to some point of view, yeah, through their point of view, and the mouth.

406
00:29:15,407 --> 00:29:24,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start realizing that mouth hasn't been speaking for a while, the body hasn't been engaging with centrality for a while, you get to discern it as a thing being there, left alone.

407
00:29:25,691 --> 00:29:28,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Finally, left alone, you get to see there, oh, this is always alone.

408
00:29:28,832 --> 00:29:31,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when I get to interfere with it, it's only because I can...

409
00:29:31,853 --> 00:29:35,095
[SPEAKER_01]: I can do so, because it's given to me as this thing that was there alone, and I can pick it up.

410
00:29:36,539 --> 00:29:38,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people don't have that relationship with their mouth.

411
00:29:38,521 --> 00:29:39,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Their mouth is their thoughts.

412
00:29:40,503 --> 00:29:42,525
[SPEAKER_01]: When they think, they speak.

413
00:29:42,545 --> 00:29:45,228
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not say anything, but I'm still thinking it through my mouth.

414
00:29:46,069 --> 00:29:48,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But why do you need to involve the perception of your mouth when you're thinking?

415
00:29:49,733 --> 00:29:50,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely gratuitous.

416
00:29:51,795 --> 00:29:58,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what you start attending to and basically like, like, you know, undoing something that was joined with a Velcro.

417
00:29:59,253 --> 00:30:00,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is your thoughts and your mouth.

418
00:30:01,394 --> 00:30:03,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, yeah, thoughts, mouth, still there, for an arahant.

419
00:30:04,514 --> 00:30:10,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But that gratuitous connection between the two, that was the basis for your action, for your consumption, for your centrality, is gone.

420
00:30:11,536 --> 00:30:20,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when you think about the body through the mouth that has been left alone, not speaking for a while, you get to see the back of your head, so to speak, as sitting there, part of this lump of the body.

421
00:30:20,739 --> 00:30:21,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not somehow in between.

422
00:30:23,668 --> 00:30:26,849
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that kind of practice would lead, then, to those later ones.

423
00:30:27,230 --> 00:30:34,253
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is an opening, as the Buddha said, you go in the direction of gliding the moon, flying in the air, in the divine air, and so on.

424
00:30:35,953 --> 00:30:37,374
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's limitless, boundless.

425
00:30:38,594 --> 00:30:44,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stop binding yourself with that which is tied to the body.

426
00:30:46,078 --> 00:30:46,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

427
00:30:47,478 --> 00:30:52,439
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just start looking at your mouth or observing sensations, because it's not about attending to your mouth.

428
00:30:52,939 --> 00:30:57,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about discerning the presence of the non-speaking mouth in regard to whatever you're attending to.

429
00:30:58,740 --> 00:31:06,762
[SPEAKER_01]: But that attention must have been rounded up and contained by the behavior purified beforehand and by the physical seclusion.

430
00:31:08,562 --> 00:31:11,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you won't be attending anything too far into the world.

431
00:31:12,214 --> 00:31:17,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get to discern the peripheral of that attention, peripheral of the non-speaking mouth, more specifically.

432
00:31:17,997 --> 00:31:22,559
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get to stop converging your thoughts onto that physical level.

433
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:27,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might not see a big deal.

434
00:31:27,321 --> 00:31:31,963
[SPEAKER_01]: It might not seem like a big deal to a mind that's still living in the central being.

435
00:31:32,423 --> 00:31:38,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But like, for example, the directionality of your thoughts and your mouth, it's completely gratuitous.

436
00:31:38,446 --> 00:31:41,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you remain bound to the sense being.

437
00:31:42,292 --> 00:31:42,612
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean...

438
00:31:42,952 --> 00:31:48,296
[SPEAKER_01]: When you think something, it's kind of... and then you say it, and you sort of... you... it's just this natural assumption.

439
00:31:48,316 --> 00:31:49,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Your thoughts control your mouth.

440
00:31:49,397 --> 00:31:53,520
[SPEAKER_01]: The thought just controls my mouth and flows into it, and now it's out there in the world.

441
00:31:53,960 --> 00:31:54,100
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

442
00:31:54,600 --> 00:31:56,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Mouth was always physical, thought was always mental.

443
00:31:56,682 --> 00:32:00,184
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot... they cannot, like, spill into each other's domains.

444
00:32:00,664 --> 00:32:01,705
[SPEAKER_01]: One is not the master of the other.

445
00:32:02,226 --> 00:32:03,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And one is not the master of the other, exactly.

446
00:32:04,227 --> 00:32:07,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize that not speaking mouth is the necessary basis for your speech.

447
00:32:09,153 --> 00:32:14,936
[SPEAKER_02]: So, say one has mastered the other, it's to assume the connection between these two completely separate things.

448
00:32:14,996 --> 00:32:17,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It's to assume the directional connection.

449
00:32:18,098 --> 00:32:21,760
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a connection in as much with thinking and pondering, speech.

450
00:32:23,021 --> 00:32:25,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Having thought and pondered, one breaks into speech.

451
00:32:25,923 --> 00:32:33,907
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, on the level of the mental domain, having had thoughts, speech appears on the level of physical domain.

452
00:32:34,728 --> 00:32:39,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But you assuming the directionality means you connecting through the sense of ownership,

453
00:32:39,757 --> 00:32:42,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, even if you don't necessarily think, my mouth is mine.

454
00:32:42,418 --> 00:32:46,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is absolutely no direction between the two.

455
00:32:46,358 --> 00:32:48,319
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not in the same space, so to speak.

456
00:32:48,619 --> 00:32:53,900
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said with the different domains, different spheres, as the Buddha would refer to.

457
00:32:54,100 --> 00:32:58,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the aggregates, as the... the... the... five different heaps.

458
00:32:58,781 --> 00:33:09,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's really not... not like... if you want... if you... if you have developed the... the... the conditions for the jhāna sufficiently, in terms of your sensory strain, withdrawal from the world, physical seclusion,

459
00:33:09,744 --> 00:33:14,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Not speaking, not chatting, just adhering to silence within that framework.

460
00:33:15,488 --> 00:33:17,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about, oh, what do I attend next?

461
00:33:17,450 --> 00:33:21,453
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, oh, what do I start discerning peripherally in regard to whatever I'm attending next?

462
00:33:22,133 --> 00:33:29,298
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is my non-speaking mouth while I'm washing the dishes, non-speaking mouth while I'm sitting quietly, non-speaking mouth while I'm looking through the window.

463
00:33:30,519 --> 00:33:37,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-speaking mouth adds a whole new layer of, like that, recognizing that separation from this lump of the body as a whole being there.

464
00:33:38,537 --> 00:33:41,398
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you stop assuming your head to be your point of view as well.

465
00:33:46,361 --> 00:33:49,082
[SPEAKER_02]: And the non-non-speaking mouth as the body.

466
00:33:50,283 --> 00:33:50,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

467
00:33:50,963 --> 00:33:53,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-speaking mouth stands for that which body is.

468
00:33:55,365 --> 00:34:01,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of body is my hand, also no, body is my feet, body is my groin, body is my belly, everything now

469
00:34:02,497 --> 00:34:04,938
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is the non-speaking mouth, the entirety of the body.

470
00:34:04,998 --> 00:34:08,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, non-speaking mouth becomes like a gateway.

471
00:34:09,779 --> 00:34:14,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a gateway for that which used to be the body we used to engage with.

472
00:34:15,300 --> 00:34:20,962
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything else that you go back to the body has to be within the container, within the doorway of the non-speaking mouth.

